  "quote_spans": [
    {
      "chunk_id": 11,
      "char_start": 1438,
      "char_end": 1789,
      "text": "After\nshe had asked him about Havre de Grace, where he had been, and about\nAmerica, where he had not been,--if his captain's wife was as fine as a\nman-of-war captain's; and then if he wouldn't like one day to marry a\nfine lady,--she wanted at last to know, from the laughing sailor lad, if\nthe officers' wives were ever allowed to be with them in war"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": 13,
      "char_start": 1124,
      "char_end": 1430,
      "text": "But she had grown marvellously handsome in that time--so much so,\nindeed, that Salve was almost taken aback when he saw her; and when they\nsaid good-bye, it was no longer in the old laughing tones, but with some\nslight embarrassment on his side--he didn't seem to know exactly how\nmatters lay between them"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": 31,
      "char_start": 0,
      "char_end": 266,
      "text": "pilot-and-his-wife\n\nhe had\nsympathised with such winning sincerity, and at the same time so\nunassumingly, in Elizabeth's grief; and when leaving assured her, with\nemotion which he made no attempt to conceal, that they owed it to her\nthat their father was still alive"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": 34,
      "char_start": 0,
      "char_end": 199,
      "text": "pilot-and-his-wife\n\nby a lantern or two out in the harbour, and a light here\nand there up in the town--sometimes standing for long minutes together,\nwith his cheek on his hand, leaning on the railing"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": 37,
      "char_start": 1781,
      "char_end": 2066,
      "text": "In the evening, when all the crew were given leave from the ship for\nthree weeks, he went off to his father instead, to see if he could learn\nmore of the situation through inquiries from him; and on the following\nMonday both were present at old Jacob's interment in Tromoe churchyard"
    }
  ],
